Code statements:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```

Code statements:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().bind(this);
```
```java
// No unregistration of the download receiver in onDestroy()
```
Reason:
The `DownloadManagerInterface` was bound to the main activity, which necessitated the unregistration of the download receiver in the `onDestroy()` method. Failing to unregister the receiver after the activity was destroyed resulted in it remaining registered, leading to potential memory leaks or crashes when the receiver attempted to interact with a non-existent activity context.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```
Reason:
The addition of the `onDestroy()` method implementation ensures that the `DownloadManagerInterface` is properly unregistered when the activity is destroyed. This prevents memory leaks and unintended behavior by ensuring that the receiver does not attempt to operate with a context that no longer exists.

